// async_engine/engine.d.rut — the async host set's engine rows
// The futures themselves never cross the boundary raw (the
// survey's law: engine-minted, compiler-owned); these rows cross the
// erasure box — `opaque(f)` seals a frame, `downcast<T>`
// recovers it — so the typed `Future<T> -> LaunchedFutureHandle<T>`
// arrow lives in rut code (the `async_host` inline package), where the
// type system can hold it.
//
// The crossings, one by one:
//   `__launch` — the launcher seals the frame from rut (`opaque(f)`);
//     the body unboxes to the frame slot and hands it to the driving
//     loop (the queue takes its own reference).
//   `__abort` — same seal; the body unboxes and flags + re-enqueues.
//   `__sleep` — the one engine-assisted mint: the body builds the sleep
//     frame and answers it SEALED under the `Future<nil>` class
//     spelling, which is what `sleep`'s `downcast<Future<nil>>`
//     recovers.
//   `__sleep_yield` — the sleep future's `Future::yield`: a bodyless
//     thunk the compiler mints whose `host_id` binds HERE. The yield
//     slot's ABI is engine-wide (every weaved async fn shares it: the
//     raw frame and cx), so the compiler mints a sealing wrapper that
//     boxes both registers before the row is reached — the wire at this
//     row is two boxes, and the body unboxes back to the raw pair.
//   `__select2` — first-ready over two sealed frames: the body unboxes
//     both, arms the select frame as their one shared waker, and parks
//     on the driving loop until one retires; the resumed yield answers
//     the winner's composite (`Either2<A, B>`, minted from the winner's
//     answer lane) and cancels the still-live loser through the same
//     cancel path `__abort` drives — the LIBRARY's law (a loser must
//     not keep running), expressed over the engine's existing rows, not
//     new machinery. The mint crosses back under the
//     `Future<Either2<A, B>>` class spelling for the wrapper's
//     downcast. Fixed arity is the boundary's law, not a choice: `host
//     fn` signatures are concrete over the crossing set. When the two
//     answers already allow it, wider reach composes in rut (see
//     async_host's `select_all`).
//   `__select_all` — the same race over a sealed cohort (`opaque` box
//     over the `[Future<T>]` array): every member is armed and launched,
//     the first retirement answers `(i, v)` sealed in the tuple, and
//     every other still-live member is cancelled (the cascade runs
//     through the members' own cancelled paths).
//   `__completer` — the manually-resolvable future primitive: the body
//     mints a cold `#frame@completer<T>` and seals it under the
//     `Future<T>` class spelling; `ty` (a `type_id<T>()` constant) is
//     the linked answer id the seal and frame names derive from.
//   `__resolve` — the resolution RIGHT's engine half: store the answer,
//     retire the frame, wake its awaiter — `__abort`-style stability
//     (false when the frame already retired). The right itself is
//     rut-side (`Completer`, a `LaunchedFutureHandle` sibling); the row
//     never sees it.
//   `__select2_yield` / `__select_all_yield` / `__completer_yield` — the
//     three competition frames' `Future::yield` thunks, bound exactly
//     like `__sleep_yield` (a sealing wrapper boxes the raw pair; the
//     row's wire is two boxes).
//
// The engine-minted types themselves (`#frame@sleep`, the cx record,
// the per-answer select/completer frames) are unspellable in rut
// source — `opaque` is the honest crossing spelling: a sealed cell the
// body recovers through the erasure box.

pub host fn __launch(f: opaque);

pub host fn __abort(f: opaque) -> bool;

pub host fn __sleep(ms: u32) -> opaque;

pub host fn __sleep_yield(f: opaque, cx: opaque);

pub host fn __select2(a: opaque, b: opaque) -> opaque;

pub host fn __select2_yield(f: opaque, cx: opaque);

pub host fn __select_all(cohort: opaque) -> opaque;

pub host fn __select_all_yield(f: opaque, cx: opaque);

pub host fn __completer(ty: u32) -> opaque;

pub host fn __resolve(f: opaque, v: opaque) -> bool;

pub host fn __completer_yield(f: opaque, cx: opaque);
